{
  "dom_id": "step-e3583",
  "name": "Example: Identifying Jump Discontinuities on a Graph when Multiple Types of Discontinuity Exist",
  "problem": "For which points does the function $y=f(x)$ below have a jump discontinuity?\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040492/assets/9e708e4fea2cd98e8d76978e58a4525d610d6f82521345b3aaa6232fd39b7539.png)",
  "worked_solution": "The function has a jump discontinuity at $x=2.$ Checking our continuity conditions, we have\n\n- $f(2)= 1$ exists $\\,\\,{\\color{green}{\\checkmark}}$\n\n- $\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\to 2}f(x)$ does not exist $\\,\\,{\\color{red}{\\times}}$\n\nSo the second condition failed. It failed because the left and right-sided limits are not equal:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to 2^-}f(x) = -1, \\qquad \\qquad \\lim_{x\\to 2^+}f(x) = 1.\n$$\n\nThese limits both exist and are finite, yet they are not equal. Therefore, the discontinuity at $x=2$ is a jump discontinuity.\n\nNote that there is also a discontinuity at $x=1,$ but this is not a jump discontinuity.",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
  "fields": [],
  "result": null,
